1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This is Task 14, reliability: idempotency and rollback.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:14,000
First, the real results of the earlier runs on a fresh server, saved unedited in the evidence folder.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Run one, the first provisioning: seven changes.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Run two, the upgrade to version one point one: five changes.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Run three, the same playbook again: zero changes.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Run four, the rollback playbook: three changes.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
And run five, the Selenium journeys on the restored release: five passed.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:45,000
A first run on a fresh server takes several minutes, so I show it from the saved evidence and run the other steps live.

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Now live.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The lab server was just provisioned in the previous task.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
I run the same playbook again.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Ansible checks every task against the real state of the server.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Everything already matches, so every task reports ok, and the play recap says changed equals zero.

14
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:18,000
That is idempotency: running the same configuration twice has the same result as running it once, and a second run is safe.

15
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,000
A health check.

16
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The portal on the lab server answers on port eighty eighty eight.

17
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The answer reports the status ok, the environment, and the version and commit that are running.

18
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
This is the release I will now replace, and later restore.

19
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Now I simulate a new release by passing a different version number, one point one point one dash demo.

20
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Ansible copies the files, records the current release as the previous one, builds the new images, and restarts the containers on the new tag.

21
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
This time several tasks report changed.

22
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
The recap shows the number of changes, and the check at the end asserts that the running version is the new one.

23
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Pretend this release is bad.

24
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
The rollback playbook returns to the previous release.

25
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:57,000
It reads the previous release file, refuses to continue if those images are missing, points the deployment at them, restarts the containers, and asserts that the health check reports exactly the target version.

26
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
No rebuild is needed, because the old images are still on the server.

27
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,000
The message says rolled back from one point one point one dash demo, to one point one point zero.

28
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Verification.

29
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,000
The health check now reports version one point one point zero again: the restored release.

30
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Both images are still stored on the server, so I could even roll forward again.

31
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
And the data lives in the same volume, so no bookings are lost.

32
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Finally I will run the Selenium journeys against this restored server, in a visible browser.


